# Spatial Continuity

## Purpose

Keep screen geography coherent across cuts: audiences build a mental map from
screen direction and eyelines, and one illegal cut breaks it. These rules are
what make generated shots cut together as a scene.

## Scope

Visual layer. Applies to blocking, shot planning, storyboards, and every
generated frame that will be cut against another. Every shot declares its
axis, screen direction, and neighbors.

## Core Principles

- Every scene establishes one or more axes (180° lines); every shot lives on
  a declared side of a declared axis.
- Screen direction is a promise: once a subject moves or looks screen-left,
  it keeps doing so until a legal change.
- Continuity is planned before generation, never patched after.

## The 180° Axis

- Each axis is recorded as `AXIS_<scene>_<letter>` (e.g. `AXIS_SC004_A`),
  defined by the line between two subjects, or a subject's path of movement.
- All coverage of an interaction MUST stay on one side of its axis. In a
  two-shot conversation, character A looks screen-right and B looks
  screen-left in every setup — reversing one of them is an axis break.
- Crossing the line is legal only via: (a) a **neutral shot** on the axis
  itself (head-on or tail-on), (b) **visible camera movement** that carries
  the audience across the line within a shot, (c) a **new axis** legally
  established when subjects move or a new interaction begins — recorded as
  the next letter (`AXIS_SC004_B`).
- Every legalized crossing MUST be recorded in the scene's continuity state
  with its method; an unrecorded crossing is a defect even if it "looks fine".

## The 30° Rule and Shot Change

- Consecutive shots of the same subject MUST change camera angle by at least
  30° or change shot size by at least one full step; otherwise the cut reads
  as a jump. Deliberate jump cuts MUST be flagged as intentional in the shot
  file.

## Screen Direction and Eyelines

- Movement direction persists across cuts: a subject exiting frame-right
  MUST enter the next shot frame-left, unless a neutral shot or an on-screen
  turn intervenes. Every shot records `enters-from` / `exits-to`.
- Journey direction (travel sequences) keeps one screen direction for the
  whole journey; reversals signal a return trip.
- Eyelines match the axis: off-screen subjects are looked at on the correct
  side and at a vertical angle consistent with relative heights and
  distances. POV shots MUST match the owning character's established
  eyeline direction.

## Validation

- Every shot file declares `axis`, `screen_direction`, `eyeline`, and
  neighbor links; `scripts/validate.js` rejects shots missing any of them.
- `scripts/check-continuity.js` walks each scene's cut order and flags axis
  side flips without a recorded legalization, 30°-rule violations derivable
  from declared angles/sizes, and enters-from/exits-to mismatches.
- `hooks/detect-continuity-drift.js` re-runs the walk when shot files change.
- Whether a generated frame actually honors its declared geometry is human
  review by the continuity-supervisor before shot approval.
